<h2 id='key-features'>⚙️ Key Features Breakdown</h2>

<h3>CRM and Contact Management</h3>
<p>GHL's CRM is pipeline-centric. You get unlimited contacts, custom fields, tagging, smart lists, and a visual pipeline board. For D2C brands, the practical move is building a pipeline that mirrors your customer journey stages — prospect, first purchase, repeat buyer, VIP — and using smart lists as the segmentation layer your email and SMS automations draw from. It works, but it requires intentional setup. The CRM does not natively surface RFM data or predicted LTV without custom field configuration or a third-party data layer.</p>

<h3>Email and SMS Marketing</h3>
<p>The email builder is drag-and-drop with a decent template library. Deliverability has improved significantly from early GHL days — the platform now supports dedicated sending domains and handles DKIM/DMARC properly. SMS is built in via Twilio infrastructure under the hood, with LC Phone (GHL's own telephony layer) as the default. Automation triggers include form submissions, tag additions, pipeline stage changes, and — with the Shopify sync active — order events like purchase, fulfillment, and refund.</p>

<h3>Funnel and Landing Page Builder</h3>
<p>The funnel builder is one of GHL's strongest features in absolute terms. It competes directly with ClickFunnels and Unbounce at a fraction of the combined cost. For D2C operators running paid acquisition, this means you can build dedicated product landing pages, upsell flows, and opt-in funnels without a separate tool. The page speed on GHL-hosted funnels is acceptable but not exceptional — we saw average Lighthouse performance scores in the mid-70s, which matters if you're running Google Ads to cold audiences.</p>

<h3>Automation Builder</h3>
<p>The workflow automation builder is visual and multi-channel. You can build sequences that combine email, SMS, internal notifications, CRM updates, webhook calls, and wait steps in a single canvas. For post-purchase flows, this is genuinely capable — we built a six-touch post-purchase sequence combining email and SMS that matched the structural complexity of what we'd built in Klaviyo, though with more manual trigger configuration required. The AI workflow assistant added in 2025 helps generate starter sequences from a plain-language prompt, which cuts initial build time meaningfully.</p>

<h3>Reputation Management</h3>
<p>GHL includes automated review request flows via SMS and email, plus a reputation dashboard that aggregates Google and Facebook reviews. For D2C brands with physical products, this is an underrated feature — triggering a review request sequence 7 days post-delivery is table stakes, and having it built into the same platform as your post-purchase email flow removes a coordination layer.</p>

<h3>Reporting and Analytics</h3>
<p>This is where GHL shows its weakest side for ecommerce operators. The native reporting covers email metrics, funnel conversion rates, appointment data, and call tracking. Ecommerce-specific reporting — revenue attributed to campaigns, cohort retention, product-level performance — is not natively available. You'll need to connect your Shopify analytics, a tool like Triple Whale or Northbeam, or build custom dashboards in a BI tool. For teams that make decisions based on email-attributed revenue, this gap is real. See our <a href='https://ecommerceoperator.io/tools/ecommerce-analytics'>ecommerce analytics tool roundup</a> for options that pair well with GHL.</p>

<div class='callout-orange'>
  <strong>⚠️ Watch Out:</strong> GHL's Shopify native sync is functional but not deep. Complex segmentation based on specific product collections, subscription status, or multi-order behavior may require Zapier, Make, or a custom webhook setup to work reliably. Factor that into your implementation timeline.
</div>

